window.SCHOOL_LISTENING={"../assets/media/b982961134d09cea4c0b5a19284da509dc7672a5cf36837b26c09e19ae4a7c9a.mp3":[{"id":"PPT_b982961134d09cea","audioId":"PPT_b982961134d09cea","title":"Book 3 · Unit 4 · RW · READING 2","subtitle":"READING 2 · source slide 160","duration":257.75,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":4.56,"text":"Protecting Our Intangible Cultural Heritage by Linda Barker","coverage":1},{"start":5.66,"end":8.34,"text":"In today's ever -changing global landscape,","coverage":1},{"start":8.78,"end":13.8,"text":"most of us recognize the importance of preserving our own unique cultural heritage.","coverage":1},{"start":14.46,"end":15.96,"text":"But what do we save?","coverage":1},{"start":16.56,"end":18.66,"text":"A magnificent building which is under threat?","coverage":1},{"start":19.38,"end":21.02,"text":"A historical part of a city?","coverage":1},{"start":21.76,"end":22.98,"text":"An important work of art?","coverage":1},{"start":23.7,"end":27,"text":"All of these are significant, but what about our songs,","coverage":1},{"start":27.34,"end":27.84,"text":"stories,","coverage":1},{"start":28.48,"end":30.3,"text":"ceremonies, and traditional practices?","coverage":1},{"start":30.96,"end":37.42,"text":"These intangible aspects of our culture can be just as meaningful as anything we build or create.","coverage":1},{"start":38.18,"end":43.2,"text":"Shouldn't they also be preserved for future generations to experience and enjoy?","coverage":1},{"start":44.08,"end":46.04,"text":"The answer, according to UNESCO,","coverage":1},{"start":46.7,"end":50.44,"text":"United Nations Educational, Scientific, and Cultural Organization,","coverage":1},{"start":50.94,"end":51.72,"text":"is yes.","coverage":1},{"start":52.4,"end":58.4,"text":"That is why in 2008 it published its first Intangible Cultural Heritage list.","coverage":1},{"start":59.06,"end":68.76,"text":"You may already know of UNESCO's famous list of World Heritage Sites, consisting of places selected for special protection because of their value to the world,","coverage":1},{"start":69.08,"end":74.38,"text":"such as the Pyramids of Giza in Egypt or the Great Barrier Reef in Australia.","coverage":1},{"start":75.06,"end":75.54,"text":"However,","coverage":1},{"start":76.08,"end":77.9,"text":"the ICH list is different.","coverage":1},{"start":78.62,"end":84.14,"text":"Its purpose is to record living customs and traditions which are endangered by globalization.","coverage":1},{"start":84.82,"end":87.62,"text":"These include languages and spoken traditions,","coverage":1},{"start":87.98,"end":89.78,"text":"such as storytelling and poetry,","coverage":1},{"start":90.36,"end":91.14,"text":"performing arts,","coverage":1},{"start":91.62,"end":92.44,"text":"traditional crafts,","coverage":1},{"start":92.86,"end":94.66,"text":"and local knowledge and beliefs.","coverage":1},{"start":95.56,"end":102.06,"text":"Today, there are more than 600 customs and traditions on the Intangible Cultural Heritage list.","coverage":1},{"start":102.94,"end":108.62,"text":"Two recent entries show us just how rich and varied are the practices selected for protection.","coverage":1},{"start":109.28,"end":111.5,"text":"The culture of the Jeju Henyo,","coverage":1},{"start":111.9,"end":114.7,"text":"the women divers of Jeju Island in South Korea,","coverage":1},{"start":115.14,"end":117.34,"text":"was added to the list in 2016.","coverage":1},{"start":118.28,"end":124.5,"text":"These amazing women dive ten meters under the sea to get food without the use of breathing equipment.","coverage":1},{"start":125.28,"end":127.9,"text":"They do this for up to seven hours a day,","coverage":1},{"start":128.24,"end":129.54,"text":"90 days of the year,","coverage":1},{"start":129.9,"end":133.02,"text":"holding their breath for about one minute every dive.","coverage":1},{"start":133.58,"end":139.72,"text":"Knowledge about diving practices is passed down from generation to generation within families.","coverage":1},{"start":140.4,"end":141.06,"text":"Yet today,","coverage":1},{"start":141.58,"end":145.9,"text":"most of the divers are in their 60s, 70s, or 80s.","coverage":1},{"start":145.9,"end":147.16,"text":"As they have become older,","coverage":1},{"start":147.74,"end":150.5,"text":"younger women have not stepped in to take their place,","coverage":1},{"start":150.84,"end":153.76,"text":"meaning the practice is in danger of disappearing.","coverage":1},{"start":155.28,"end":161.8,"text":"Another cultural practice added to the Intangible Cultural Heritage list in 2021 is falconry.","coverage":1},{"start":162.37,"end":166.48,"text":"This is the ancient custom of hunting with trained birds of prey,","coverage":1},{"start":166.8,"end":168.88,"text":"such as falcons and hawks.","coverage":1},{"start":169.18,"end":171.64,"text":"Regarded as both an art form and a sport,","coverage":1},{"start":172.1,"end":175.04,"text":"it requires years of training and discipline.","coverage":1},{"start":176.12,"end":182.2,"text":"Falconry is practiced around the world, from France to Mongolia to the United Arab Emirates,","coverage":1},{"start":182.52,"end":188.66,"text":"which is home to the world's first falcon hospital and each year hosts a falcon beauty contest.","coverage":1},{"start":188.66,"end":189.96,"text":"Sadly,","coverage":1},{"start":190.54,"end":195.38,"text":"falconry is now threatened by the growth of cities and the loss of natural habitats.","coverage":1},{"start":196.14,"end":201.72,"text":"There are fears that the practice may disappear unless efforts to preserve it are successful.","coverage":1},{"start":203.04,"end":213.02,"text":"Both Jeju -Henyo and falconry illustrate the tremendous range of cultural practices which the Intangible Cultural Heritage list was created to preserve.","coverage":1},{"start":213.54,"end":214.24,"text":"And yet,","coverage":1},{"start":214.62,"end":218.04,"text":"while most of us would agree that UNESCO's efforts are valuable,","coverage":1},{"start":218.72,"end":224.44,"text":"I find myself questioning the fairness and usefulness of the Intangible Cultural Heritage list.","coverage":1},{"start":225.26,"end":229.5,"text":"Why are some customs and practices included, while others are not?","coverage":1},{"start":230.1,"end":231.22,"text":"Who decides this?","coverage":1},{"start":231.94,"end":235.14,"text":"Does inclusion on the list make a difference in the long term?","coverage":1},{"start":235.82,"end":238.36,"text":"In this age of rapid globalization,","coverage":1},{"start":238.98,"end":243.22,"text":"when fewer young people are interested in learning about the customs of their ancestors,","coverage":1},{"start":243.82,"end":249.48,"text":"it may already be too late to preserve much of our diverse and precious cultural heritage.","coverage":1},{"start":250.38,"end":252.6,"text":"But for the benefit of future generations,","coverage":1},{"start":253.24,"end":255.84,"text":"I believe we must never stop trying.","coverage":1}],"file":"73f0101327a1606ecfaa.js","track":0,"media":"../assets/media/b982961134d09cea4c0b5a19284da509dc7672a5cf36837b26c09e19ae4a7c9a.mp3"}],"../assets/media/4f4cd9cbf244d67ae9dda224ff7f38a8061d984e9fec4dd27afeb6ac9116771c.mp3":[{"id":"PPT_4f4cd9cbf244d67a","audioId":"PPT_4f4cd9cbf244d67a","title":"Book 3 · Unit 4 · RW · READING 1","subtitle":"READING 1 · source slide 48","duration":305.48,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":3.56,"text":"Customs Around the World by Andy Schmidt.","coverage":1},{"start":5,"end":6.12,"text":"In recent decades,","coverage":1},{"start":6.56,"end":10.46,"text":"foreign travel has become a multi -billion dollar industry.","coverage":1},{"start":11.4,"end":13.9,"text":"International travel has many benefits,","coverage":1},{"start":14.38,"end":21.24,"text":"but visitors can run into trouble if they don't know some basic rules about the cultures they're visiting.","coverage":1},{"start":22,"end":34.86,"text":"It is very important for travelers to take the time to learn about the cultures they plan to visit so that they know what to expect and how to avoid cultural misunderstandings.","coverage":1},{"start":35.66,"end":40.02,"text":"That is why we are presenting this Customs Around the World series,","coverage":1},{"start":40.44,"end":47.12,"text":"where we will look at three different cultures every month to help you become a well -informed traveler.","coverage":1},{"start":48.08,"end":51.4,"text":"This month's exciting destinations are Brazil,","coverage":1},{"start":51.82,"end":52.38,"text":"Japan,","coverage":1},{"start":53.02,"end":54.32,"text":"and Saudi Arabia.","coverage":1},{"start":55.44,"end":56.28,"text":"Brazil.","coverage":1},{"start":57.66,"end":58.46,"text":"In general,","coverage":1},{"start":58.92,"end":60.86,"text":"Brazilian culture is informal.","coverage":1},{"start":61.66,"end":66.5,"text":"Most Brazilians are very friendly people, so it is important to say","coverage":1},{"start":66.5,"end":67,"text":"hello","coverage":1},{"start":67,"end":69.5,"text":"and goodbye to the people you meet.","coverage":1},{"start":70.56,"end":71.12,"text":"Normally,","coverage":1},{"start":71.46,"end":74.44,"text":"women kiss men and each other on the cheek,","coverage":1},{"start":75.02,"end":77.9,"text":"but men usually just shake hands.","coverage":1},{"start":78.96,"end":84.58,"text":"Brazilians typically stand very close to each other and touch each other's arms,","coverage":1},{"start":84.98,"end":88.02,"text":"elbows, and back regularly while speaking.","coverage":1},{"start":89.06,"end":91.66,"text":"Even if this is unusual in your culture,","coverage":1},{"start":92.08,"end":94.78,"text":"try not to move away if this happens.","coverage":1},{"start":95.76,"end":97.56,"text":"If you go to a business meeting,","coverage":1},{"start":97.96,"end":100.36,"text":"you are not expected to take a gift.","coverage":1},{"start":101.1,"end":101.74,"text":"In fact,","coverage":1},{"start":102.18,"end":105.72,"text":"an expensive gift can be seen as suspicious.","coverage":1},{"start":106.64,"end":108.48,"text":"On the other hand,","coverage":1},{"start":108.8,"end":112.9,"text":"if you are invited to someone's house, you should take a gift,","coverage":1},{"start":113.48,"end":114.3,"text":"for example,","coverage":1},{"start":114.66,"end":116.18,"text":"flowers or chocolate.","coverage":1},{"start":117.24,"end":117.76,"text":"However,","coverage":1},{"start":118.2,"end":120.58,"text":"avoid anything purple or black,","coverage":1},{"start":120.94,"end":123.52,"text":"as these colors are related to death.","coverage":1},{"start":124.48,"end":126.72,"text":"If you are invited to dinner,","coverage":1},{"start":127.14,"end":129.26,"text":"arrive at least 30 minutes late,","coverage":1},{"start":129.8,"end":135.62,"text":"but always dress well, because a person's appearance can be very important to Brazilians.","coverage":1},{"start":138.14,"end":142.22,"text":"The Japanese are quite different from the Brazilians.","coverage":1},{"start":142.84,"end":144.64,"text":"They tend to be quite formal,","coverage":1},{"start":144.96,"end":147.14,"text":"so don't stand too close.","coverage":1},{"start":147.82,"end":151.08,"text":"Kissing or touching in public is not common.","coverage":1},{"start":151.08,"end":154.24,"text":"When you meet Japanese people socially,","coverage":1},{"start":154.66,"end":156.38,"text":"they may shake your hand.","coverage":1},{"start":156.78,"end":157.3,"text":"However,","coverage":1},{"start":157.8,"end":160.78,"text":"bowing, bending forwards to show respect,","coverage":1},{"start":161.34,"end":162.62,"text":"is the traditional greeting.","coverage":1},{"start":164.2,"end":165.36,"text":"In a business meeting,","coverage":1},{"start":165.72,"end":172.22,"text":"the Japanese often like to know what your position is in your company before they talk to you.","coverage":1},{"start":172.22,"end":176.54,"text":"You should hand over a business card using both hands,","coverage":1},{"start":177.14,"end":179.08,"text":"and when you receive a business card,","coverage":1},{"start":179.52,"end":181.72,"text":"you should immediately read it carefully.","coverage":1},{"start":182.72,"end":184.76,"text":"It is important to be punctual.","coverage":1},{"start":185.46,"end":188.4,"text":"You should arrive early and dress formally.","coverage":1},{"start":189.36,"end":190.86,"text":"Gifts are often exchanged,","coverage":1},{"start":191.32,"end":196.52,"text":"but the recipient may refuse the gift at least once before accepting it.","coverage":1},{"start":197.32,"end":200.76,"text":"You should remember to do the same if you receive a gift.","coverage":1},{"start":200.76,"end":203.04,"text":"When you present your gift,","coverage":1},{"start":203.36,"end":206.72,"text":"you should say that it is a token of your appreciation.","coverage":1},{"start":208.54,"end":213.09,"text":"Saudi Arabia is a very traditional country.","coverage":1},{"start":214.1,"end":215.46,"text":"In business situations,","coverage":1},{"start":215.92,"end":219.96,"text":"you should know that hierarchy is important to Saudis.","coverage":1},{"start":220.18,"end":220.74,"text":"Therefore,","coverage":1},{"start":221.16,"end":225.76,"text":"it is important to greet the oldest or the most senior person first.","coverage":1},{"start":226.76,"end":230.4,"text":"Men may shake hands with men and women with women,","coverage":1},{"start":230.74,"end":232.8,"text":"always using the right hand.","coverage":1},{"start":233.58,"end":237.62,"text":"Men and women do not touch or shake hands in public.","coverage":1},{"start":239.12,"end":243.06,"text":"Personal relationships are very important in Saudi Arabia.","coverage":1},{"start":244,"end":245.06,"text":"In a business meeting,","coverage":1},{"start":245.5,"end":248.16,"text":"do not start with business matters immediately.","coverage":1},{"start":249.02,"end":249.58,"text":"Instead,","coverage":1},{"start":249.92,"end":253.18,"text":"start by asking about people's family or health.","coverage":1},{"start":253.18,"end":256.48,"text":"Arrive on time for business meetings,","coverage":1},{"start":257,"end":260.04,"text":"but do not be surprised if others are late.","coverage":1},{"start":261,"end":267.36,"text":"Punctuality is less important in Saudi Arabia than it is in Japan or the United States.","coverage":1},{"start":268.06,"end":269.64,"text":"Business dress is formal.","coverage":1},{"start":270.24,"end":272.58,"text":"Men should wear dark suits and ties,","coverage":1},{"start":273.1,"end":275.34,"text":"and women should wear modest clothes.","coverage":1},{"start":275.34,"end":283.9,"text":"Gifts are not expected at business meetings until those involved have formed a strong relationship.","coverage":1},{"start":284.96,"end":288.16,"text":"If you are invited to a Saudi home or office,","coverage":1},{"start":288.84,"end":291.52,"text":"acceptable gifts are good quality chocolate,","coverage":1},{"start":292.14,"end":292.46,"text":"coffee,","coverage":1},{"start":292.88,"end":293.56,"text":"or dates.","coverage":1},{"start":294.54,"end":298.1,"text":"Be careful not to admire the things the host owns,","coverage":1},{"start":298.6,"end":303.58,"text":"because he or she will feel obliged to give an item to you as a gift.","coverage":1}],"file":"d4bf0f643758dde200fc.js","track":0,"media":"../assets/media/4f4cd9cbf244d67ae9dda224ff7f38a8061d984e9fec4dd27afeb6ac9116771c.mp3"}]};
window.SCHOOL_SOURCE_ANSWERS=[{"text":"1 At what age are people in your country allowed to buy a house, drive a car, get married, own a credit card, or ride a motorcycle? Possible answer: In my country, people can drive a car at the age of 18.","slide":4,"shape":"16","bookPage":"82"},{"text":"2 Why do you think this age was chosen? Possible answer: This age may have been chosen because people need enough maturity and responsibility.","slide":4,"shape":"17","bookPage":"82"},{"text":"3 Do you think the age should be older or younger? Why / Why not? Possible answer: I think the age should stay the same because people can learn responsibility before they reach 18.","slide":4,"shape":"18","bookPage":"82"},{"text":"4 What do you think coming of age means? At what age does it happen in your country? Possible answer: Coming of age means becoming an adult and taking on new rights and responsibilities.","slide":5,"shape":"16","bookPage":"82"},{"text":"1  The ceremony includes a special clothing ritual.","slide":13,"shape":"7","bookPage":"82"},{"text":"2  Adults have legal rights as well as duties.","slide":13,"shape":"8","bookPage":"82"},{"text":"3  Looking after the family is an important responsibility.","slide":13,"shape":"9","bookPage":"82"},{"text":"4  The traditions date back over 1,000 years.","slide":13,"shape":"10","bookPage":"82"},{"text":"5  They wear formal clothes on important occasions.","slide":13,"shape":"11","bookPage":"82"},{"text":"Exercise 2 Correct activities: giving presents; wearing traditional clothes; taking part in a ceremony; taking photos.","slide":16,"shape":"23","bookPage":"83"},{"text":"Exercise 3 What are the differences between the modern and traditional celebrations of coming-of-age day in South Korea? Possible answer: Modern celebrations include flowers, often 20 roses, and perfume. Traditional celebrations include hair-changing and clothing rituals and bowing to parents.","slide":16,"shape":"24","bookPage":"83"},{"text":"1  South Korean 19-year-olds celebrate becoming adults on the same day.","slide":19,"shape":"6","bookPage":"83"},{"text":"2  The gift of perfume has a special meaning.","slide":19,"shape":"7","bookPage":"83"},{"text":"3  The hair-changing ceremony is over 1,000 years old.","slide":19,"shape":"8","bookPage":"83"},{"text":"4  Young women wear a jade hairpin and a crown with many ornaments.","slide":19,"shape":"9","bookPage":"83"},{"text":"5  Young men use a net to put their hair in a topknot.","slide":19,"shape":"10","bookPage":"83"},{"text":"6  The young people bow to their parents.","slide":19,"shape":"11","bookPage":"83"},{"text":"1  ornament — an object that is beautiful rather than useful","slide":26,"shape":"7","bookPage":"83"},{"text":"2  bow — to bend your head or body forward to show respect","slide":26,"shape":"8","bookPage":"83"},{"text":"3  crown — a circle-shaped decoration for the head, often made of gold and jewels","slide":26,"shape":"9","bookPage":"83"},{"text":"4  topknot — long hair tied up high on the head","slide":26,"shape":"10","bookPage":"83"},{"text":"1 What traditions of this event have stayed the same? Why? Possible answer: Families still meet and share a special meal because spending time together matters.","slide":28,"shape":"16","bookPage":"83"},{"text":"2 How has the modern celebration changed? Do you think the changes are good or bad? Why? Possible answer: Some people now send online greetings. This is convenient, although it may feel less personal.","slide":28,"shape":"17","bookPage":"83"},{"text":"6  I always spend a lot of time on my hair and clothes before a special occasion. I want to\nmake sure my appearance is perfect.","slide":39,"shape":"7","bookPage":"84"},{"text":"7  In most cultures, people who have a close family relationship enjoy spending time together\nand giving each other gifts.","slide":39,"shape":"8","bookPage":"84"},{"text":"What rules do visitors to your country need to know in order to be polite? Greeting: Possible answer: In my community, people usually shake hands when they meet someone for the first time.","slide":42,"shape":"16","bookPage":"85"},{"text":"What rules do visitors to your country need to know in order to be polite? Giving gifts: Possible answer: Visitors often bring a small gift when they are invited to a person's home.","slide":42,"shape":"17","bookPage":"85"},{"text":"What rules do visitors to your country need to know in order to be polite? Behaviour in business meetings: Possible answer: People usually arrive on time and listen before they speak.","slide":42,"shape":"18","bookPage":"85"},{"text":"What rules do visitors to your country need to know in order to be polite? Business dress code: Possible answer: People often wear formal clothes for an important meeting.","slide":43,"shape":"19","bookPage":"85"},{"text":"What rules do visitors to your country need to know in order to be polite? Punctuality: Possible answer: It is polite to arrive a few minutes early for an appointment.","slide":43,"shape":"20","bookPage":"85"},{"text":"1 Share the information from your tables. Which rules from your tables are similar? Which are different? Possible answer: Our communities both value polite greetings, but the greetings themselves can be different.","slide":46,"shape":"16","bookPage":"85"},{"text":"2 The article discusses correct behaviour in Brazil, Japan and Saudi Arabia. What do you know about the specific customs of these countries? Possible answer: I think the article may compare greetings, gifts and behaviour in meetings.","slide":46,"shape":"17","bookPage":"85"},{"text":"In recent decades, foreign travel has become a multi-billion dollar industry. International travel has many benefits, but visitors can run into trouble if they don’t know some basic ‘rules’ about the cultures they’re visiting. It is very important for travellers to take the time to learn about the cultures they plan to visit so that they know what to expect and how to avoid cultural misunderstandings.","slide":61,"shape":"6","bookPage":"86"},{"text":"That is why we are presenting this ‘Customs around the world’ series, where we will look at three different cultures every month to help you become a well-informed traveller. This month’s exciting destinations are Brazil, Japan and Saudi Arabia.","slide":62,"shape":"5","bookPage":"86"},{"text":"In general, Brazilian culture is informal. Most Brazilians are very friendly people, so it is important to say hello and goodbye to the people you meet. Normally women kiss men and each other on the cheek, but men usually just shake hands. Brazilians typically stand very close to each other and touch each other’s arms, elbows and back regularly while speaking.","slide":63,"shape":"5","bookPage":"86"},{"text":"Even if this is unusual in your culture, try not to move away if this happens. If you go to a business meeting, you are not expected to take a gift. In fact, an expensive gift can be seen as suspicious.","slide":64,"shape":"5","bookPage":"86"},{"text":"On the other hand, if you are invited to someone’s house, you should take a gift – for example, flowers or chocolate. However, avoid anything purple or black, as these colours are related to death.","slide":65,"shape":"5","bookPage":"86"},{"text":"If you are invited to dinner, arrive at least 30 minutes late, but always dress well, because a person’s appearance can be very important to Brazilians.","slide":66,"shape":"5","bookPage":"86"},{"text":"The Japanese are quite different from the Brazilians. They tend to be quite formal, so don’t stand too close. Kissing or touching in public is not common. When you meet Japanese people socially, they may shake your hand. However, bowing, bending forwards to show respect, is the traditional greeting.","slide":67,"shape":"5","bookPage":"86"},{"text":"In a business meeting, the Japanese often like to know what your position is in your company before they talk to you. You should hand over a business card using both hands, and when you receive a business card, you should immediately read it carefully. It is important to be punctual. You should arrive early and dress formally. Gifts are often exchanged, but the recipient may refuse the gift at least once before accepting it.","slide":68,"shape":"5","bookPage":"86"},{"text":"You should remember to do the same if you receive a gift. When you present your gift, you should say that it is a token of your appreciation.","slide":69,"shape":"5","bookPage":"86"},{"text":"Saudi Arabia is a very traditional country. In business situations you should know that hierarchy is important to Saudis. Therefore, it is important to greet the oldest or the most senior person first. Men may shake hands with men, and women with women, always using the right hand. Men and women do not touch or shake hands in public.","slide":70,"shape":"5","bookPage":"86"},{"text":"Personal relationships are very important in Saudi Arabia. In a business meeting, do not start with business matters immediately. Instead, start by asking about people’s family or health. Arrive on time for business meetings, but do not be surprised if others are late. Punctuality is less important in Saudi Arabia than it is in Japan or the US. Business dress is formal.","slide":71,"shape":"5","bookPage":"86"},{"text":"Men should wear dark suits and ties and women should wear modest clothes.","slide":72,"shape":"5","bookPage":"86"},{"text":"Gifts are not expected at business meetings until those involved have formed a strong relationship. If you are invited to a Saudi home or office, acceptable gifts are good-quality chocolate, coffee or dates. Be careful not to admire the things the host owns, because he or she will feel obliged to give an item to you as a gift.","slide":73,"shape":"5","bookPage":"86"},{"text":"1  Arriving on time means being punctual.","slide":75,"shape":"7","bookPage":"86"},{"text":"2  A system of different levels of importance is a hierarchy.","slide":75,"shape":"8","bookPage":"86"},{"text":"3  A very unusual gift may seem suspicious.","slide":75,"shape":"9","bookPage":"86"},{"text":"4  A small thank-you gift can be a token of your appreciation.","slide":75,"shape":"10","bookPage":"86"},{"text":"Not mentioned: e — table manners.","slide":90,"shape":"13","bookPage":"85"},{"text":"Custom / behaviour\nBrazil\nJapan\nSaudi Arabia\nGreeting\nWomen kiss other women & men. Men shake hands with other men.\nOK to shake hands, but bowing is more traditional.\nGreet the oldest or most senior person first. Women shake hands with women, men with men.","slide":110,"shape":"13","bookPage":"87"},{"text":"Custom / behaviour\nBrazil\nJapan\nSaudi Arabia\nGifts\nBring a gift to someone's house.\nReceiving: Common to refuse before you accept. Giving: Say it is just a token of your appreciation.\nNot expected at business meetings. Can bring chocolate, coffee or dates.","slide":111,"shape":"14","bookPage":"87"},{"text":"Custom / behaviour\nBrazil\nJapan\nSaudi Arabia\nBusiness behaviour\nDo not bring a gift.\nHand over your business card with both hands. When you receive a business card, immediately read it carefully.\nDo not start with business matters immediately. Ask about people's family or health.","slide":112,"shape":"14","bookPage":"87"},{"text":"Custom / behaviour\nBrazil\nJapan\nSaudi Arabia\nDress / appearance\nDress well.\nDress formally.\nBusiness dress is formal.","slide":113,"shape":"14","bookPage":"87"},{"text":"Custom / behaviour\nBrazil\nJapan\nSaudi Arabia\nPunctuality\nArrive at least 30 minutes late.\nArrive early.\nArrive on time.","slide":114,"shape":"13","bookPage":"87"},{"text":"1 In Brazil, why would people probably be suspicious of an expensive gift? Possible answer: They might think the giver expects a favour or is trying to influence them.","slide":129,"shape":"17","bookPage":"87"},{"text":"2 Why should not you move away if Brazilians touch you during conversation? Possible answer: Touching may signal friendliness, so moving away could seem unfriendly.","slide":129,"shape":"18","bookPage":"87"},{"text":"3 Why would Japanese business people want to know your position in a company? Possible answer: Their behaviour may depend on your status in the company.","slide":129,"shape":"19","bookPage":"87"},{"text":"4 What could be the reason why it is important to greet the oldest person first in Saudi Arabia? Possible answer: Age may be linked to respect and social importance.","slide":130,"shape":"20","bookPage":"87"},{"text":"5 What can be the negative result of a cultural misunderstanding? Possible answer: Someone may feel offended, and a personal or business relationship may suffer.","slide":130,"shape":"21","bookPage":"87"},{"text":"11. Paragraph A\nAnswer: iv","slide":133,"shape":"5","bookPage":null},{"text":"12. Paragraph B\nAnswer: ii","slide":133,"shape":"8","bookPage":null},{"text":"1 In Mexican culture, a quinceañera is a special ceremony to celebrate a girl's 15th birthday. a formal event b traditional gift c month. a formal event.","slide":150,"shape":"14","bookPage":"88"},{"text":"2 In some cultures, several generations live together in the same house — parents, grandparents and children. a unrelated people b close friends c groups of people. c groups of people.","slide":150,"shape":"15","bookPage":"88"},{"text":"3 Children tend to have the same beliefs as their parents when they are young, but this may change when they become adults. a the way someone looks b experiences c ideas you are sure about. c ideas you are sure about.","slide":150,"shape":"16","bookPage":"88"},{"text":"4 Japanese cities are trying to preserve some of their beautiful old buildings so people can enjoy them in the future. a complete b keep the same c replace. b keep the same.","slide":151,"shape":"14","bookPage":"88"},{"text":"5 It is a Chinese tradition to give money to young people in red envelopes at New Year. a story that parents tell their children b practical idea c something that a community has done for a long time. c something that a community has done for a long time.","slide":151,"shape":"15","bookPage":"88"},{"text":"6 We should consider all the valuable aspects of culture, including songs, dance, storytelling and other similar practices. a shared events b parts of a situation or subject c special times or places. b parts of a situation or subject.","slide":151,"shape":"16","bookPage":"88"},{"text":"7 Places which are listed as UNESCO World Heritage sites have special protection and cannot be destroyed. a way of keeping something safe b new idea c money for support. a way of keeping something safe.","slide":152,"shape":"18","bookPage":"88"},{"text":"Read the title. What do you think it means? What will the article be about? Possible answer: The article may explain how to protect cultural practices, knowledge and traditions.","slide":157,"shape":"17","bookPage":"89"},{"text":"What is happening in the photos? How do you think they are related to the title? Possible answer: One photo shows a diver; the other shows a person with a trained bird.","slide":157,"shape":"18","bookPage":"89"},{"text":"1  Traditional songs are intangible: they exist, but we cannot touch them.","slide":159,"shape":"6","bookPage":"91"},{"text":"2  Traditions from the past that remain important are part of our heritage.","slide":159,"shape":"7","bookPage":"91"},{"text":"In today’s ever-changing global landscape, most of us recognize the importance of preserving our own unique cultural heritage. But what do we save? A magnificent building which is under threat? A historical part of a city? An important work of art? All of these are significant, but what about our songs, stories, ceremonies and traditional practices? These intangible aspects of our culture can be just as meaningful as anything we build or create. Shouldn’t they also be preserved for future generations to experience and enjoy?","slide":171,"shape":"17","bookPage":"89"},{"text":"The answer, according to UNESCO (United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization), is yes. That is why in 2008 it published its first Intangible Cultural Heritage (ICH) list. You may already know of UNESCO’s famous list of World Heritage Sites, consisting of places selected for special protection because of their value to the world, such as the Pyramids of Giza in Egypt or the Great Barrier Reef in Australia. However, the ICH list is different. Its purpose is to record living customs and traditions which are endangered by globalization. These include languages and spoken traditions (such as storytelling and poetry), performing arts, traditional crafts and local knowledge and beliefs.","slide":172,"shape":"20","bookPage":"89"},{"text":"Today, there are more than 600 customs and traditions on the ICH list. Two recent entries show us just how rich and varied are the practices selected for protection. The culture of the Jeju Haenyeo, the women divers of Jeju Island in South Korea, was added to the list in 2016. These amazing women dive ten metres under the sea to get food –without the use of breathing equipment. They do this for up to seven hours a day, 90 days of the year, holding their breath for about one minute every dive. Knowledge about diving practices is passed down from generation to generation within families. Yet today, most of the divers are in their 60s, 70s or 80s. As they have become older, younger women have not stepped in to take their place, meaning the practice is in danger of disappearing.","slide":173,"shape":"20","bookPage":"89"},{"text":"Another cultural practice added to the ICH list in 2021 is falconry. This is the ancient custom of hunting with trained birds of prey, such as falcons and hawks. Regarded as both an art form and a sport, it requires years of training and discipline. Falconry is practised around the world from France to Mongolia to the United Arab Emirates, which is home to the world’s first falcon hospital and each year hosts a falcon ‘beauty contest’. Sadly, falconry is now threatened by the growth of cities and the loss of natural habitats. There are fears that the practice may disappear unless efforts to preserve it are successful.","slide":174,"shape":"20","bookPage":"89"},{"text":"Both Jeju Haenyeo and falconry illustrate the tremendous range of cultural practices which the ICH list was created to preserve. And yet, while most of us would agree that UNESCO’s efforts are valuable, I find myself questioning the fairness and usefulness of the ICH list. Why are some customs and practices included while others are not? Who decides this? Does inclusion on the list make a difference in the long term? In this age of rapid globalization, when fewer young people are interested in learning about the customs of their ancestors, it may already be too late to preserve much of our diverse and precious cultural heritage. But for the benefit of future generations, I believe we must never stop trying.","slide":175,"shape":"20","bookPage":"89"},{"text":"1  Paragraph 2  Possible answer: d — The ICH was established to protect cultural traditions that are in danger.","slide":188,"shape":"6","bookPage":"89"},{"text":"2  Paragraph 3  Possible answer: c — Diving has a long tradition, but young people are not learning this skill.","slide":188,"shape":"7","bookPage":"89"},{"text":"3  Paragraph 4  Possible answer: a — Falconry is popular in many countries, but cities are taking over habitats.","slide":188,"shape":"8","bookPage":"89"},{"text":"4  Paragraph 5  Possible answer: e — The value and fairness of the ICH list is not always clear.","slide":188,"shape":"9","bookPage":"89"},{"text":"a — The article discusses a way of recording the world's intangible cultural heritage.","slide":190,"shape":"13","bookPage":"89"},{"text":"1  The Great Barrier Reef is an example of intangible cultural heritage.  F — It is a World Heritage Site, a physical place, not an intangible practice.","slide":202,"shape":"6","bookPage":"90"},{"text":"2  The women divers of Jeju Island can hold their breath for three minutes.  F — They hold their breath for about one minute per dive.","slide":202,"shape":"7","bookPage":"90"},{"text":"3  Most younger women are not interested in becoming divers.  T — Younger women have not stepped in to replace the older divers.","slide":202,"shape":"8","bookPage":"90"},{"text":"4  There is a falcon hospital in the United Arab Emirates.  T — The UAE is home to the world's first falcon hospital, according to the text.","slide":202,"shape":"9","bookPage":"90"},{"text":"5  Only a few falcon habitats remain in the world.  DNS — The text says habitats are being lost, but gives no number or proportion remaining.","slide":202,"shape":"10","bookPage":"90"},{"text":"1 What is the difference between tangible and intangible culture? Possible answer: Tangible culture includes physical objects and places; intangible culture includes practices, knowledge and traditions.","slide":204,"shape":"17","bookPage":"90"},{"text":"2 Why does the author say that globalization is threatening these traditions? Possible answer: Global influences may reduce younger people's interest in local practices, so skills may not be passed on.","slide":204,"shape":"18","bookPage":"90"},{"text":"3 The author says that entries on the ICH list are 'rich and varied'. What other kinds of traditions do you think are on the list? Possible answer: Traditional songs, dances, storytelling and craft skills are possible examples.","slide":204,"shape":"19","bookPage":"90"},{"text":"4 Does the author believe UNESCO's work is important? How do you know? Possible answer: Yes. She calls the efforts valuable and says we must never stop trying.","slide":205,"shape":"17","bookPage":"90"},{"text":"We tend to tip the waiter in restaurants.","slide":209,"shape":"14","bookPage":"92"},{"text":"Brazilian culture tends to be informal.","slide":209,"shape":"15","bookPage":"92"},{"text":"Looking into another person's eyes can seem rude.","slide":209,"shape":"16","bookPage":"92"},{"text":"Common hand gestures like waving can be misunderstood in a different culture.","slide":210,"shape":"18","bookPage":"92"},{"text":"In the Middle East, many old people live with their children.","slide":210,"shape":"19","bookPage":"92"},{"text":"Brazilian men usually shake hands, though it is not unusual for men to kiss each other on the cheek.","slide":215,"shape":"17","bookPage":"93"},{"text":"Cultural knowledge is frequently helpful in business situations.","slide":215,"shape":"18","bookPage":"93"},{"text":"In Japan, you should always arrive on time for an appointment.","slide":215,"shape":"19","bookPage":"93"},{"text":"In my community, families usually meet for important celebrations.","slide":218,"shape":"19","bookPage":"93"},{"text":"People often greet neighbours when they meet.","slide":218,"shape":"20","bookPage":"93"},{"text":"Visitors sometimes bring a small gift when they visit a friend.","slide":218,"shape":"21","bookPage":"93"},{"text":"The business meeting was brief, and we went straight to the reception for our overseas visitors.","slide":225,"shape":"14","bookPage":"93"},{"text":"The loss of our cultural heritage is a serious problem.","slide":225,"shape":"15","bookPage":"93"},{"text":"In many cultures, parents and their married children live in separate houses.","slide":225,"shape":"16","bookPage":"93"},{"text":"The ICH list has played a huge part in protecting cultural traditions.","slide":226,"shape":"18","bookPage":"93"},{"text":"Some customs and traditions are not obvious to people who are visiting a country for the first time.","slide":226,"shape":"19","bookPage":"93"},{"text":"My family has a tradition of eating together on Sunday. We meet at my grandparents’ house. We talk about the week and share food. I think this time is important.","slide":230,"shape":"6","bookPage":null},{"text":"At my cousin’s graduation, the students wore blue gowns. Families sat in a large hall. The principal gave a speech. Everyone clapped for the graduates.","slide":230,"shape":"9","bookPage":null},{"text":"1. The course aimed to make the whole class professional boat builders.\nAnswer: FALSE","slide":235,"shape":"5","bookPage":null},{"text":"2. Each pair's first making task was a full-sized boat.\nAnswer: FALSE","slide":235,"shape":"8","bookPage":null},{"text":"11. Paragraph A\nAnswer: iii","slide":238,"shape":"5","bookPage":null},{"text":"12. Paragraph B\nAnswer: iv","slide":239,"shape":"5","bookPage":null},{"text":"My family once sent cards for every birthday. Now we usually call relatives online. This saves time when people live far away. I still keep cards from older relatives.","slide":244,"shape":"6","bookPage":null},{"text":"My town should preserve its small library. Many generations have used it. The building has a quiet reading room. It is probably worth keeping.","slide":244,"shape":"9","bookPage":null},{"text":"Paragraph\nMain idea\nDetails\nMy response\n1 Introduction\nIntangible cultural heritage is worth preserving.\nSongs, stories, ceremonies and practices matter as much as physical heritage.\n","slide":262,"shape":"13","bookPage":"94"},{"text":"Paragraph\nMain idea\nDetails\nMy response\n2\nICH list was established to protect living customs and practices.\nList includes languages, spoken traditions, performing arts, crafts, knowledge and beliefs.\n","slide":263,"shape":"15","bookPage":"94"},{"text":"Paragraph\nMain idea\nDetails\nMy response\n3\nThe Jeju diving tradition is in danger.\nOlder women dive without breathing equipment; younger women are not replacing them.\n","slide":264,"shape":"15","bookPage":"94"},{"text":"Paragraph\nMain idea\nDetails\nMy response\n4\nFalconry is a valuable practice facing threats.\nIt is both art and sport; expanding cities and habitat loss threaten it.\n","slide":265,"shape":"15","bookPage":"94"},{"text":"Paragraph\nMain idea\nDetails\nMy response\n5 Conclusion\nPreservation is valuable, despite doubts about the list.\nThe author questions fairness and long-term effects but says we must keep trying.\n","slide":266,"shape":"15","bookPage":"94"},{"text":"Paragraph\nMain idea\nDetails\nMy response\n1 Introduction\nIntangible cultural heritage is worth preserving.\nSongs, stories, ceremonies and practices matter as much as physical heritage.\nI agree that cultural value is not limited to physical objects.","slide":283,"shape":"15","bookPage":"95"},{"text":"Paragraph\nMain idea\nDetails\nMy response\n2\nICH list was established to protect living customs and practices.\nList includes languages, spoken traditions, performing arts, crafts, knowledge and beliefs.\nRecording a practice may help people learn about it, but people also need opportunities to practise it.","slide":284,"shape":"15","bookPage":"95"},{"text":"Paragraph\nMain idea\nDetails\nMy response\n3\nThe Jeju diving tradition is in danger.\nOlder women dive without breathing equipment; younger women are not replacing them.\nPassing skills to younger people seems essential for survival.","slide":285,"shape":"15","bookPage":"95"},{"text":"Paragraph\nMain idea\nDetails\nMy response\n4\nFalconry is a valuable practice facing threats.\nIt is both art and sport; expanding cities and habitat loss threaten it.\nProtecting skills may also require protecting the environments they depend on.","slide":286,"shape":"15","bookPage":"95"},{"text":"Paragraph\nMain idea\nDetails\nMy response\n5 Conclusion\nPreservation is valuable, despite doubts about the list.\nThe author questions fairness and long-term effects but says we must keep trying.\nI agree that the effort should continue, with clearer selection and support for communities.","slide":287,"shape":"15","bookPage":"95"},{"text":"Original: Brazilians typically stand very close to each other and touch each other's arms, elbows and back regularly while speaking. (Schmidt, 2018) Paraphrase: According to Schmidt (2018), Brazilians tend to stand very close to each other and touch each other's arms, elbows and back regularly while speaking. Strategy: c — use indirect speech","slide":299,"shape":"19","bookPage":"96"},{"text":"Original: Brazilians typically stand very close to each other and touch each other's arms, elbows and back regularly while speaking. (Schmidt, 2018) Paraphrase: Brazilian people tend to stand quite close to one another, and they frequently touch each other on the upper body while they are talking (Schmidt, 2018). Strategy: a — use synonyms","slide":299,"shape":"20","bookPage":"96"},{"text":"Original: Brazilians typically stand very close to each other and touch each other's arms, elbows and back regularly while speaking. (Schmidt, 2018) Paraphrase: While they are talking, Brazilian people tend to stand very close to one another and touch each other's backs, arms or elbows frequently (Schmidt, 2018). Strategy: b — change the order of words or phrases","slide":300,"shape":"19","bookPage":"96"},{"text":"Original: 1 [The purpose of the ICH list] is to record living customs and traditions which are endangered by globalization. (Barker, 2018). Possible paraphrase: The aim of the ICH list is to document living practices and traditions that are at risk because of globalization (Barker, 2018).","slide":304,"shape":"13","bookPage":"96"},{"text":"Original: 2 Sadly, falconry is now threatened by the growth of cities and the loss of natural habitats (Barker, 2018). Possible paraphrase: The expansion of urban areas and disappearance of natural environments unfortunately put falconry at risk today (Barker, 2018).","slide":304,"shape":"14","bookPage":"96"},{"text":"Original: 3 In this age of rapid globalization, when fewer young people are interested in learning about the customs of their ancestors, it may already be too late to preserve much of our diverse and precious cultural heritage (Barker, 2018). Possible paraphrase: Barker (2018) suggests that saving many valuable and varied traditions may already be impossible because globalization is advancing quickly and fewer young people want to learn their ancestors' customs.","slide":305,"shape":"16","bookPage":"96"},{"text":"Compare your paraphrases with a partner. Possible feedback: Your version keeps the original claim and the citation. In the sentence about falconry, “urban expansion” and “disappearing natural habitats” replace the original wording clearly. In the final sentence, keep “may” so that the claim does not become more certain than the source.","slide":310,"shape":"18","bookPage":"96"},{"text":"1 Which sentence restates the main idea in Reading 1? Possible answer: The first sentence of the summary.","slide":335,"shape":"16","bookPage":"98"},{"text":"2 Compare the main idea sentence in Reading 1 and the main idea sentence of the summary paragraph. Which paraphrasing strategies did the summary writer use? Possible answer: All three strategies: synonyms, changed word order and reporting language.","slide":335,"shape":"17","bookPage":"98"},{"text":"3 What other information from Reading 1 did the writer include in the summary paragraph? Possible answer: The three countries and selected customs concerning greetings, space, gifts, business and punctuality.","slide":335,"shape":"18","bookPage":"98"},{"text":"4 Which examples from Reading 1 did the writer include in the summary? Do you think this is too much, too little or the right amount of detail? Possible answer: Examples include touching, business cards and asking about a host's family or health.","slide":336,"shape":"20","bookPage":"98"},{"text":"5 How many references to the author are there in the summary paragraph? Underline them. Possible answer: Four: Schmidt (2018) says; Schmidt focuses; According to the author; The author stresses.","slide":336,"shape":"21","bookPage":"98"},{"text":"6 Where does the writer give her opinion? Underline the sentence. Possible answer: At the beginning of the second paragraph: I definitely agree with Schmidt's main point: it is easy to make a mistake if you don't know about other people's customs.","slide":337,"shape":"19","bookPage":"98"},{"text":"7 How does the writer support her opinion? Possible answer: A personal example about researching a suitable gift before visiting a home in India.","slide":337,"shape":"20","bookPage":"98"},{"text":"8 How does the writer conclude the essay? What transition does she use? Possible answer: The writer restates the importance of learning customs before travelling. The transition is In short.","slide":337,"shape":"21","bookPage":"98"},{"text":"In the article 'Customs around the world', Schmidt (2018) says it is important for travellers to learn about the customs of other countries in order to prevent cultural misunderstandings in the places they are visiting. Schmidt focuses on three countries — Brazil, Japan and Saudi Arabia — and describes some customs visitors should know about. According to the author, Brazilians are informal people. They touch a lot and punctuality is not expected on most occasions. Japanese culture is exactly the opposite.","slide":338,"shape":"5","bookPage":"97"},{"text":"People don't touch in public, and there are rules for how to exchange business cards, how to dress, what kind of gifts to give and when to arrive at a meeting or someone's home. Saudi culture is also formal. For example, women and men are not allowed to touch each other in public. The author stresses the importance of personal relationships and warns us that it is important to spend time asking about a host's family or health, before starting to talk about business matters.","slide":339,"shape":"5","bookPage":"97"},{"text":"I definitely agree with Schmidt's main point: it is easy to make a mistake if you don't know about other people's customs. To give an example from my own experience, I was travelling in India when I was invited to dinner at a friend's home. I had never been to an Indian home, so I decided to do some reading about a proper gift to bring.","slide":340,"shape":"5","bookPage":"97"},{"text":"One of the things I learnt is that a guest should not bring white flowers, because white is associated with death. Reading this information before the event prevented me from making an embarrassing mistake. In short, these examples remind us that travellers must study the customs of a new country before visiting it.","slide":341,"shape":"5","bookPage":"97"},{"text":"The town has moved its festival to the park so more families can attend. This change has made the event easier to join. I think the new location is a good idea. However, organizers should still protect the traditional parts of the festival.","slide":353,"shape":"6","bookPage":null},{"text":"Some families use video greetings because they can speak to relatives abroad. The note shows that technology has changed one tradition. I think video calls are helpful for distant families. Still, a written card may be meaningful on a special occasion.","slide":354,"shape":"6","bookPage":null}];
